Yes. You must do everything shown if possible, though you can do it in any order.
Usually, there's no downside: Reclaiming, gaining Energy/Power Cards, and adding Presence are almost strictly good. (You can always reposition a Presence already on the island when adding Presence [p. 14], so "running out" isn't a concern.) Spirits with "Move a Presence up to Range N" Growth effects can Move it 0. However, a few Spirits do include things you'll have to work around, including:
- Ocean's Hungry Grasp often requires you to Push or Gather Presence;
- Heart of the Wildfire often adds Blight along with Presence, so being required to add Presence can be dangerous;
- Downpour Drenches the World and Ember-Eyed Behemoth both discard Power Cards as part of one of their Growth options;
- Breath of Darkness Down Your Spine must let pieces escape the Endless Dark.
- Several Spirits have Growth options that add Presence to particular land types, which could force Presence outside of that land type to be repositioned accordingly in the unlikely case where the Spirit has no Presence left on Presence tracks and no Presence on the island is already in that land type (else it could be repositioned into the land it's already in). Note that "a land with your Presence" can't cause this problem, since that allows any Presence to be repositioned into a land its already in.
If you can't do something in a Growth option, do as much as you can and skip the rest – you're free to pick Growth options where you can't successfully complete all of the instructions. (E.g., if you Push Presence from each Ocean, and there's a board where none of your Presence is in the Ocean, you simply don't Push Presence on that board.)
Nevertheless, you can't skip paying an explicit "cost" for a Growth option, which must be paid in full order to pick that option and perform the associated actions.

Yes, so long as all of the Damage is being done during the same use of the Power.
For example, if a Spirit uses "Vigor of the Breaking Dawn" with its threshold and pushes Dahan into two different lands (dealing Damage in three lands), they could:
- Deal +3 Damage in one land;
- Deal +2 Damage in one land and +1 Damage in another land;
- Get +1 Damage in each of the three lands.
When using a Power like "Vengeance of the Dead" that deals Damage multiple times later in the turn in response to something else happening, the bonus applies only one of those times (though the bonus can still be split across multiple lands if you reached the threshold of "Vengeance of the Dead").

No, resolve the triggered action(s) after the entire step is over, once you've finished resolving every card in that step. If the triggered action references the corresponding cards, it refers to all of those cards.
So, for example, when up against Habsburg Monarchy (Livestock Colony) Level 1+ and have both a "Jungles" card and a "Mountains+Wetlands" card in the Build Space, then (in order):
- The Invaders build in Jungles;
- The Invaders build in Mountains and Wetlands;
- "Migratory Herders" triggers, gathering Towns from Sands into each Jungle, Mountain, and Wetland.

Yes, the ones that use those words as part of a different instruction – or as part of no instruction at all! The Violence Aspect checks for Powers that actively do Damage or Destroy things, while An Ominous Dawn and Wavering Resolve check for Powers that add Fear, not just reference Fear as a concept.
The Violence Aspect has no special interactions with Powers that:
- Change how or how much Damage is done by something else, like Concealing Shadows and Flame's Fury;
- Save pieces from destruction, like Birds Cry Warning and Flowing and Silent Forms Dart By;
- Check when pieces are destroyed, like Portents of Disaster;
- Interact with damaged/destroyed pieces, like Gift of Abundance and Vanish Softly Away, Forgotten by All; or
- Only include "Damage" or "Destroy" in their reminder text, like Dread Apparitions and Stubborn Solidity
(Some Power Cards, like Instruments of Their Own Ruin, fit into one or more of the above categories but also Damage or Destroy pieces, so the Violence Aspect can use its extra plays on them.)
As of the release of Nature Incarnate, there are no Power Cards with the Fear symbol that don’t generate Fear, so An Ominous Dawn and Wavering Resolve have no sneaky exceptions. Also see this FAQ about Gift of Wind-Sped Steps, which has similar conditions, but for Gather and Push.

No, the original pieces were saved from destruction by that action, so they can't be chosen for further Damage or Destroy instructions even in their new form. The Transforming Aspect can't, e.g., deal 2 Damage to a Town with Heart of the Wildfire's "Firestorm" Innate Power, Push the resulting Explorers to another one of its lands with Blight, and then use remaining "Firestorm" Damage to harm them there.
This protection remains even if another action was responsible for the replacement. For example, if Volcano Looming High uses "Explosive Eruption" against the Tsardom of Russia Level 2+, and it:
- Destroys an Explorer with one of the early thresholds, which is saved from destruction by Russia's Level 2, "A Sense for Impending Disaster";
- Adds Blight with the third threshold, flipping the "Thriving Communities" Blight Card; and
- Replaces that saved Explorer with a Town due to "Thriving Communities";
then the resulting Town is still safe from any remaining "Explosive Eruption" Damage from the fourth threshold.

No, the Strife still counts as being in the land for Ravage Actions unless some effect is explicitly ignoring Strife.
In particular:
- The Fear Card "Beset by Many Troubles" still grants Defend based on that Strife;
- The Event "Stricken" still skips the Ravage Action in that land;
- The Event "Cooperation Among Towns" doesn't grant bonus Damage if any Towns/Cities are still participating in that Ravage;
- Dahan in that land don't participate in Ravage if they're near the Incarna of Wandering Voice Keens Delirium (though this is also true for other reasons)

Effects that can't resolve:
- Anything that interacts with adjacent lands, including Push and Gather (since there are no adjacent lands);
- Invader Actions;
- Anything conditional on having a particular terrain or being Coastal;
- Anything that references the board that target land is on.
Effects that do resolve, but generally not in a useful way:
- Defend and other Damage penalties to Invaders;
- Isolate;
- Skipping Invader Actions.
All other standard effects (Fear, Damage, Destroy, Remove, Replace, Add, Upgrade, Downgrade) work as they normally would, albeit with the exception that adding Blight doesn't destroy Presence or cascade, since the Endless Dark isn't a land. (Thematically, there isn't a real ecosystem to damage.)
The Endless Dark counts as "target land" for the purpose of resolving any of the Power's effects, since that's meant to refer to the place you are targeting rather than being an additional check if that place is a land.

Breath's Power can target The Endless Dark if they do any of the following:
- Have no targeting restrictions;
- Only target Inland lands (e.g., "Here There Be Monsters"), since the Endless Dark is not adjacent to the Ocean;
- Targets all lands except a specific terrain (e.g., "Dry Wood Explodes in Smoldering Splinters"), since the Endless Dark has no terrain;
- Check for pieces that are currently in The Endless Dark (e.g., "Territorial Strife" targeting The Endless Dark if a City is present there); or
- Specifically target The Endless Dark (e.g., "Lost in the Endless Dark").
Breath can't target The Endless Dark with Powers that target specific terrains or Coastal lands, check for pieces that aren't currently in The Endless Dark, or that have origin requirements that Breath doesn't satisfy (e.g., "The Jungle Hungers" if Breath has no Presence in Jungles).

Any time you have resolved the Power, including:
- The original use of the Power;
- Any time Relentless Punishment repeated that Power;
- Any time that Power repeated itself (e.g., Winds of Rust and Atrophy repeating itself with its threshold); and
- Any time something else repeated that Power (e.g., Powerstorm)
Thus, if at all possible, you want to target the land where you're going to use Relentless Punishment before you use any other Repeats.

All Blight added by the individual Ravage Action that deals 6+ overkill to the land, including:
- The 1 Blight normally added by dealing 2+ Damage to the land in Ravage;
- Blight added by cascades, including both cascades caused by an unusual condition, like the "Fragile Sands" effect of the Varied Terrains scenario, and "chain" cascades caused by cascading into a land that already has Blight;
- Blight added by the Ravage due to the Invader Damage meeting a particular condition (e.g., The Kingdom of Sweden's "Heavy Mining" check).
However, it excludes:
- Blight added by a separate action "After" the Ravage Action, like from The Kingdom of Scotland's Level 5 "Runoff and Bilgewater" effect;
- Blight added from box instead of the the Blight Card, like via the "Self-Sacrifice Buys Time" Event or Stone's Unyielding Defiance using "Hold the Island Fast with a Bulwark of Will."
